course: dictionary
lesson: 2342
title: Crustaceans: shelled animals with jointed legs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A crustacean is an animal with a hard outer shell, jointed legs and no backbone. Most crustaceans live in water. Crabs, lobsters, shrimp, prawns and crayfish are all crustaceans. Scientists put them in a group because they share these features.
explain: Read the clues in the sentence to work out the meaning. «A woodlouse has 14 legs and is a crustacean, related to crabs.» The clue is related to crabs. Crabs are crustaceans, so a woodlouse is one too, even though it lives on land.
explain: Crustaceans have no backbone, so they belong to the group called invertebrates. Insects and spiders are invertebrates too. «Water fleas are tiny see-through crustaceans that jump through water.» The word see-through and the word water are clues that help you picture them.
fact: A crustacean is an animal with a hard outer shell, jointed legs and no backbone.
fact: Crabs, lobsters, shrimp, prawns and crayfish are all crustaceans.
fact: «Woodlice» are crustaceans that live on land, not in water.
fact: Crustaceans grow by shedding their shell, which is called moulting.
fact: Some people are allergic to crustaceans, which are a kind of shellfish.
exercise: Which of these is a crustacean? | *Prawn | Earthworm | Snail
exercise: «Woodlice are not insects; they are crustaceans, relatives of crabs, with fourteen legs.» How many legs does a woodlouse have? | Eight | *Fourteen | Ten | Six
exercise: Ruby says a crustacean has a backbone like a fish. Is she right? | Yes, like a fish | Yes, but only the big ones | It depends on the water | *No, a crustacean has no backbone
test: Which animal is NOT a crustacean? | Crayfish | Crab | Shrimp | *Earthworm
test: Why are woodlice surprising crustaceans? | They can fly | They have a backbone | *They live on land, and most crustaceans live in water | They have no legs
exam: A shrimp, a prawn and a crab are all crustaceans; where do they usually live? | Holes in the ground | Deserts | Trees | *Water
